1
00:00:00,258 --> 00:00:05,374
Rotational Atherectomy, or Rotablation,
in Calcified Coronary Disease,

2
00:00:05,424 --> 00:00:10,031
explained by Dr. Mohd Maqbool
Sohil, Interventional Cardiologist.

3
00:00:10,458 --> 00:00:13,434
The coronary arteries
supply the heart muscle.

4
00:00:13,979 --> 00:00:19,203
Here, a heavily calcified lesion narrows
the proximal left anterior descending,

5
00:00:19,253 --> 00:00:20,860
or LAD, artery.

6
00:00:21,251 --> 00:00:23,189
Cut-away view: superficial,

7
00:00:23,239 --> 00:00:27,084
concentric calcium lies in the
intima, next to the lumen.

8
00:00:27,677 --> 00:00:30,689
The media and adventitia lie outside it.

9
00:00:31,124 --> 00:00:35,446
Rigid calcium can stop balloons
from crossing or expanding fully,

10
00:00:35,517 --> 00:00:39,827
risking stent underexpansion, so
the lesion is modified first.

11
00:00:40,258 --> 00:00:42,877
Intravascular imaging grades calcium.

12
00:00:43,446 --> 00:00:47,839
Thick, long and wide arcs of
calcium predict underexpansion:

13
00:00:47,946 --> 00:00:56,434
for example, an arc > 180°, thickness
> 0.5 mm, and length > 5 mm.

14
00:00:56,838 --> 00:00:57,778
Step one.

15
00:00:58,419 --> 00:01:00,442
A guide catheter is engaged.

16
00:01:01,109 --> 00:01:06,226
A dedicated 0.009-inch rotational
atherectomy guidewire,

17
00:01:06,276 --> 00:01:11,288
floppy or extra-support, is advanced
across the lesion to the distal vessel.

18
00:01:11,604 --> 00:01:12,414
Step two.

19
00:01:13,102 --> 00:01:15,076
A diamond-coated elliptical burr on a

20
00:01:15,126 --> 00:01:18,242
flexible drive shaft is
advanced over the wire,

21
00:01:18,292 --> 00:01:20,911
with its sheath, through
the guide catheter.

22
00:01:21,324 --> 00:01:30,622
Burr sizing: a burr-to-artery ratio of about
0.5–0.6, typically a 1.25–1.5 mm burr.

23
00:01:31,215 --> 00:01:34,250
The burr is parked just
proximal to the lesion.

24
00:01:34,604 --> 00:01:35,545
Step three.

25
00:01:36,126 --> 00:01:43,197
Activation at a platform speed
of about 140,000–180,000 rpm,

26
00:01:43,257 --> 00:01:48,292
with continuous flush of saline plus
a vasodilator and heparin cocktail.

27
00:01:48,624 --> 00:01:53,910
Gentle pecking advancement in
short runs, of about 15–20 s.

28
00:01:54,491 --> 00:01:59,848
Avoid speed drops greater than 5,000
rpm, and never force the burr.

29
00:02:00,291 --> 00:02:03,765
Differential cutting: the burr
ablates inelastic calcium,

30
00:02:03,815 --> 00:02:07,386
while elastic, healthy tissue
deflects away from it.

31
00:02:07,671 --> 00:02:08,576
Step four.

32
00:02:09,216 --> 00:02:11,609
Ablation produces microparticles,

33
00:02:11,740 --> 00:02:16,083
mostly smaller than 10 µm,
smaller than red blood cells,

34
00:02:16,133 --> 00:02:20,633
which are carried downstream and cleared
by the reticuloendothelial system.

35
00:02:21,004 --> 00:02:25,123
Slow-flow and no-reflow risk
is minimised by short runs,

36
00:02:25,254 --> 00:02:28,195
pauses between runs, and continuous flush.

37
00:02:28,672 --> 00:02:32,636
A supportive, coaxial guide
sized for the burr is essential.

38
00:02:33,181 --> 00:02:38,015
Burr entrapment is rare, and is best
prevented by never forcing the burr.

39
00:02:39,624 --> 00:02:42,374
Result: a smoother, enlarged lumen.

40
00:02:42,967 --> 00:02:47,527
IVUS shows a thinned, modified
calcium arc with small fractures.

41
00:02:47,938 --> 00:02:48,890
Step five.

42
00:02:49,447 --> 00:02:55,578
Lesion preparation is completed:
over a 0.014-inch workhorse wire,

43
00:02:55,650 --> 00:03:00,245
a non-compliant balloon dilates the
modified lesion, fracturing calcium.

44
00:03:00,638 --> 00:03:01,590
Step six.

45
00:03:02,254 --> 00:03:05,647
A drug-eluting stent is
deployed and well expanded.

46
00:03:06,242 --> 00:03:10,004
IVUS confirms good stent
expansion and apposition.

47
00:03:10,351 --> 00:03:11,256
Key points.

48
00:03:11,860 --> 00:03:17,513
Indications: severe coronary calcification,
uncrossable or undilatable lesions,

49
00:03:17,563 --> 00:03:20,872
and lesion preparation to
allow full stent expansion.

50
00:03:21,468 --> 00:03:26,025
Technique: a dedicated
0.009-inch guidewire,

51
00:03:26,075 --> 00:03:30,229
gentle pecking in short runs,
and a continuous flush cocktail.

52
00:03:30,765 --> 00:03:34,122
Complications to watch:
slow flow or no-reflow,

53
00:03:34,206 --> 00:03:37,325
perforation, dissection,
and burr entrapment.

54
00:03:37,791 --> 00:03:42,970
This animation is for education and is
not a substitute for medical advice.

